I'm writing an ArrayList contract test with Pex. However, I'm facing a problem with the setup of the test project. The compilation of the project is failing because it can not find the reference of "PexAPIWrapper".
What should be done to setup the test project to compile successfully?

A: It seems like PexAPIWrapper is not being found. Could you provide more details? How are you including the PexAPIWrapper in your test project? 

If PexAPIWrapper is a custom namespace, it may not be recognized by the compiler as it's not a standard/built-in library. You would need reference it as a project or assembly. Do you have the PexAPIWrapper project setup somewhere that's not included in your current project? Also, ensure that it's added as a reference in your test project. 

If you added it as a project make sure that project gets build before yours. In case you added it as a DLL, ensure the DLL is in your test project's bin directory or in your project's references. Also, the DLL should be a .NET Assembly and it should support the right version of the .NET runtime that your project uses.

Hope this helps to ensure a successful test project compilaiton. Let me know if you encounter any other problems.

Also, your test class file must not have the same name as the .cs file since in the build process, all the CS files are squished into a single DLL which makes it difficult for Pex to differentiate one test class from another and hence, leading to failures in execution. Make sure the names do not clash. Your test file's name should end with "Tests". E.g., if your main file was ArrayListTest.cs then your test class should be ArrayListTestTests.cs. Please rename your test file accordingly if necessary. 

Hope this helps to ensure a successful test project compilation. Let me know if you encounter any other problems.

I understand the issue could be due to PexAPIWrapper reference not properly added. 
If you have the source code of PexAPIWrapper, you should build it first and then add reference to this DLL in your project. 
If you only have the .dll file, make sure your test project is referencing to the .dll file.

I hope it helps. If you can provide more information about your project configuration and the PexAPIWrapper library, I could give even more specific advice. 

Pex is designed to understand your public API without any additional information. That's why you define your test classes and methods as they should be visible externally. So we cannot "Pex" into the private members of the tested class.

Just ensure PexAPIWrapper is a public class or PexAPIWrapper is a public method or property you are testing. The reason here is that Pex is trying to verify that your class behaves correctly when the public interface is used. So Pex will not be able to test if these private methods or properties are working correctly which are being exposed through your public API. 

So, ensure that your PexAPIWrapper is public and all methods are declared public as well. Or if those methods/properties are meant to be exposed like this, then that's your responsibility to add those methods/properties to your class in order to test them as part of your public interface.

If the issue still persists, please provide details about the PexAPIWrapper library so that I could assist you better. – 

Pex cannot test the internal methods, properties or classes. Since the behavior of the internal methods is not specified in the documentation (i.e., a test can't be written about their behavior), if you test these methods with Pex all the methods will pass irrespective of the actual internal implementation.

In other words – Pex is designed around the idea that a class's internal state can be considered opaque to the user and only the class's public contract matters, i.e., what methods it exposes. Therefore everything inside the class is regarded as an implementation detail and isn't tested with Pex. 

Make sure every internal method and property you want to test is added to the public interface. This--------------------	    //@@@ If the return value is false, new list's last index of x is equals old list's last index of x
